<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles Fourier</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1772">1772</a>–<a href="/years/1837">1837</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as François Marie Charles Fourier</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles Fourier was a French philosopher regarded as the founder of utopian socialism. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/besancon">Besançon</a>, he worked for years as a travelling salesman and correspondence clerk; his first book, the Theory of the Four Movements, appeared anonymously in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> in 1808 and sold few copies. He held that cooperation was the key to social success and proposed communities called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanxes</a>, housed in buildings called phalanstères, where jobs were assigned by interest and desire. He also called trade, which he associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, the source of all evil. His ideas inspired intentional communities such as Brook Farm and the North American Phalanx.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-fourier.jpg" alt="Charles Fourier" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean Gigoux · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_painting_of_Charles_Fourier_by_Jean_Gigoux_—_1835_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Charles Fourier</b> is credited with coining “féminisme” in 1837, but no trace of the word has been found in his works.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/feminism">Feminism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The first modern currents, later called utopian socialism, are associated with Henri de Saint-Simon, <b>Charles Fourier</b> and Robert Owen.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/socialism">Socialism · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
